; An `open` with a note after it and no currency constraint — both legal
; beancount, and together they were read as one.
;
; The currency is optional there, so the reader took whatever followed the
; account as one: with a comment and nothing else, that is `;`. Nothing
; refused it, and it surfaced three directives later out of `create_account`
; as `Cannot find commodity (CURRENCY, ;)` — a complaint about a commodity,
; on a line that names none.
;
; Stripping the comment leaves the real question, which is what this fixture
; is now for: an `open` with no currency at all. Beancount means "any
; currency" by it and a GnuCash account is kept in exactly one, so there is
; nothing to write — and the tool says so, naming the line and what to add.
;
; What this fixture produces: a refusal quoting the directive.
;
; Verified by tests/integration/test_beancount_reads_what_a_person_writes.py.
2024-01-01 commodity CAD
    gnucash-mnemonic: "CAD"
    gnucash-namespace: "CURRENCY"
    gnucash-fullname: "Canadian Dollar"
    gnucash-fraction: "100"
2024-01-01 open Assets CAD
    gnucash-name: "Assets"
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111a"
    gnucash-type: "Asset"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"
2024-01-01 open Assets:Bank  ; opened in March
    gnucash-name: "Assets:Bank"
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111b"
    gnucash-type: "Bank"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"
2024-01-01 open Expenses CAD
    gnucash-name: "Expenses"
    gnucash-guid: "1111111111111111111111111111111c"
    gnucash-type: "Expense"
    gnucash-placeholder: "False"

2024-02-01 * "Grocer" "Lunch"
    gnucash-guid: "2222222222222222222222222222222a"
  Expenses 50.00 CAD
  Assets:Bank -50.00 CAD
